THE CALL OF THE WILD

Credit: Written by

Author: Jack London

Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London

Draft date: 7/30/2026

Notes: Period live-action medium throughout; Yukon Gold Rush, 1897. Buck is a huge tawny-and-white dog, part St. Bernard, part Scotch shepherd, weighing roughly 140 lbs, with a heavy coat that grows shaggier as the film progresses.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

A sprawling Santa Clara Valley estate: white house, wide verandas, stables, orchards, a cement swimming tank glinting in the sun. BUCK, a huge tawny St. Bernard-shepherd cross, bounds across the lawn ahead of two boys, king of the grounds.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every tide-water dog, strong of muscle and warm of coat, from Puget Sound to San Diego.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Over Judge Miller's place Buck ruled. Here he was born, and here he had lived the four years of his life.

MANUEL, a gardener's helper, watches Buck from the hedge, glancing furtively toward the road.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But Buck did not know that Manuel, one of the gardener's helpers, was an undesirable acquaintance. Manuel loved to play Chinese lottery, and the wages of a gardener's helper do not lap over the needs of a wife and numerous progeny.

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - ORCHARD - NIGHT

Manuel leads Buck by the collar through the dark orchard toward the road. Buck trots along trustingly. At a small flag station a STRANGER waits, coins already in hand.

Money passes between Manuel and the Stranger. The Stranger loops a length of rope around Buck's neck under the collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

The Stranger jerks the rope tight. Buck growls, then springs — the Stranger grapples him by the throat and throws him down, twisting the rope until Buck's tongue lolls and his eyes glaze.

A distant train whistle shrieks. The two men haul Buck's limp body toward the tracks.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

Buck wakes, jolted by the rattling car, his tongue swollen and aching. His eyes open into fury. He lunges — his jaws close on the Stranger's hand before he is choked senseless again.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Concerning that night's ride, the man spoke most eloquently for himself.

EXT. SALOON BACKYARD, SAN FRANCISCO - NIGHT

A small shed behind a saloon. The Stranger nurses a bloodied, bandaged hand. The SALOON-KEEPER, a bulging-faced man, looks him over.

SALOON-KEEPER
How much did the other mug get?

STRANGER
A hundred. Wouldn't take a sou less, so help me.

SALOON-KEEPER
That makes a hundred and fifty. And he's worth it, or I'm a squarehead.

They pry a heavy brass collar from Buck's neck by force, then shove him into a narrow crate. Buck lies in the dark, nursing his wrath.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He did not know why, but he felt oppressed by the vague sense of impending calamity.

EXT. SEATTLE HIGH-WALLED YARD - DAY

Four men carry the crate into a small enclosed yard. A stout man — RED SWEATER MAN, sagging red sweater, calm eyes — signs a delivery book. He fetches a hatchet and a club.

RED SWEATER MAN
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck hurls himself at the widening gap in the crate. He springs — the club meets him mid-air and crushes him to the ground. Again and again he charges; again and again the club drops him, until he lies bleeding and senseless in the snow.

Red Sweater Man kneels, pats the battered head gently.

RED SWEATER MAN
Well, Buck, my boy — we've had our little ruction, and the best thing we can do is let it go at that. You've learned your place, and I know mine.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, he knew that, but he was not broken. That club was a revelation. It was his introduction to the reign of primitive law.

Buck drinks water eagerly from the man's hand, then bolts a meal of raw meat, chunk by chunk.

EXT. SEATTLE HIGH-WALLED YARD - DAY (LATER)

Days pass — other dogs arrive in crates, are broken the same way. A weazened little man, PERRAULT, French-Canadian courier for the Canadian Government, appraises Buck.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

RED SWEATER MAN
Three hundred, and a present at that.

Money changes hands. Perrault leads Buck and CURLY, a good-natured Newfoundland, toward the docks.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That was the last he saw of the man in the red sweater, and the last he saw of the warm Southland.

INT. NARWHAL, TWEEN-DECKS - DAY

Belowdecks on the steamer. FRANCOIS, a swarthy French-Canadian half-breed, and Perrault look over the growing team: Curly, DAVE — gaunt, morose, uninterested in anything — and SPITZ, a big white Spitzbergen dog who smiles as he steals from Buck's food. Francois's whip cracks across Spitz before Buck can retaliate.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That was fair of Francois, Buck decided, and the half-breed began his rise in Buck's estimation.

Later, on deck, snow falls for the first time. Buck sniffs at it, licks it from his paw, startled as it vanishes on his tongue. Onlookers laugh.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Chaos of dogs, sleds, and men. Curly approaches a husky in friendly greeting. The husky strikes — a flash of teeth — and rips her face open. Forty huskies close in a silent circle.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So sudden was it, and so unexpected. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you.

Francois wades in with an axe; men with clubs scatter the pack. Curly lies torn and lifeless in the trampled snow. Buck watches, and something in him hardens.

Francois buckles a harness onto Buck.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
As he had seen horses work, so he was set to work.

Buck hauls firewood beside Dave and Sol-leks, learning "ho" and "mush" under the sting of Francois's whip.

EXT. DYEA TRAIL CAMP - NIGHT

Buck is chased out of the tent by curses and flying pans. Alone in the cold, he wanders, shivering, until he finds BILLEE curled snug beneath the snow. He digs his own nest and sleeps.

By morning three more huskies join the team — JOE, sour and snarling, BILLEE, appeasing and good-natured, and SOL-LEKS, "the Angry One," blind in one eye.

FRANCOIS
Wot I say? Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Montage: the sled team toils up the Dyea Canon, over Chilcoot Divide, across frozen lakes. Buck grows lean and hard.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was ravenous. He never had enough, and suffered from perpetual hunger pangs.

Buck watches PIKE steal a slice of bacon unseen. The next day, Buck does the same — and DUB, blundering, takes the blame.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This first theft marked Buck as fit to survive. It marked the decay of his moral nature — a vain thing in the Northland, under the law of club and fang.

At night, Buck points his nose at a star and howls, long and wolflike.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his ancestors, dead and dust, pointing nose at star and howling down through the centuries and through him.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

A bleak camp on bare ice. Buck returns from his ration to find Spitz occupying his snow-nest. A warning snarl — and for the first time, Buck attacks.

FRANCOIS
Gif it to heem, by Gar! Gif it to heem, the dirty t'eef!

Before the fight can finish, the camp erupts — dozens of starving huskies pour in from the dark, ravaging the grub-box, tearing at the team. Buck and Spitz are forced to break off and fight side by side against the invaders.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Never had Buck seen such dogs — mere skeletons, draped loosely in draggled hides.

The team flees onto the lake ice, wounded but alive.

EXT. THIRTY MILE RIVER - DAY

The team edges along cracking rim ice. Perrault breaks through and is saved by his pole; Dave and Buck plunge through and are hauled out half-frozen. A hard-won mile is gained by nightfall.

EXT. PELLY RIVER TRAIL - DAY

Harnessing up, DOLLY suddenly howls and lunges at Buck, foaming — gone mad. Buck flees for his life in a wide, desperate loop, Dolly a leap behind. Francois's axe falls, ending it.

Buck staggers to the sled, exhausted — and Spitz seizes the moment, tearing at his unresisting flank before Francois's lash drives him off.

PERRAULT
One devil, dat Spitz. Some dam day heem keel dat Buck.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. Some dam fine day heem get mad lak hell an' chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow.

EXT. TRAIL CAMP - NIGHT

Buck deliberately shields PIKE from Spitz's discipline, sparking open mutiny. The team's order frays night by night — bickering, shirking, Francois cursing and tearing his hair.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck was too clever ever again to be caught red-handed. He worked faithfully in the harness — yet it was a greater delight slyly to precipitate a fight amongst his mates.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK - NIGHT

Moonlight on snow. The team runs down a snowshoe rabbit in full cry — sixty dogs strong, Buck at the head, ablaze with the joy of the chase. Spitz cuts across the bend and kills the rabbit first.

Buck does not cry out. He drives into Spitz, shoulder to shoulder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In a flash Buck knew it. The time had come. It was to the death.

They circle in the ghostly silence, the watching pack drawn into a ring. Fang meets fang. At last Buck sweeps low, breaks Spitz's foreleg, then the other. The circle of eyes tightens.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was no hope for him. Mercy was a thing reserved for gentler climes.

Buck springs in. The dark circle closes. Spitz vanishes from the moon-flooded snow. Buck stands over the empty place, the dominant primordial beast, his kill made good.

EXT. TRAIL CAMP - DAWN

Francois discovers Spitz gone, Buck wounded and victorious.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Wot I say? I spik true w'en I say dat Buck two devils.

Francois brings Sol-leks forward to lead. Buck drives him off and stands in the place himself. Francois drags him back with a club; Buck circles just out of reach, refusing all work until his claim is granted.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops it. Buck trots in, laughing, and takes his place at the head of the team.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The reorganized team flies down the trail — a record run, forty miles a day, fourteen days running. Buck rules with a discipline Spitz never achieved.

In Skaguay, Perrault and Francois are met with cheers. Francois kneels, throws his arms around Buck, and weeps.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And that was the last of Francois and Perrault. Like other men, they passed out of Buck's life for good.

EXT. MAIL TRAIL - DAY

A SCOTCH HALF-BREED drives Buck's team now, hauling the mail — heavier loads, harder miles, month after grinding month.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a monotonous life, operating with machine-like regularity. Eighteen hundred miles, with but five days' rest.

By the campfire at night, Buck blinks dreamily at the flames — and sees, in his mind, a short, hairy, half-naked man crouched by another fire in another age, fearful of the dark.

Dave grows sick and gaunt, whimpering when Sol-leks is put in his old place. He bites through the traces to reclaim it, pulls until he collapses, and crawls toward the harness one last time.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For the pride of trace and trail was his, and sick unto death, he could not bear that another dog should do his work.

The half-breed walks back behind the timber. A single revolver shot. Buck and every dog know what has happened.

EXT. SKAGUAY DOCKS - DAY

The mail team, worn to skeletons, drags into Skaguay. Government agents sell them off. Two men — HAL, twenty, bristling with cartridges, and CHARLES, middle-aged, weak-eyed — buy Buck's team "for a song," alongside Hal's sister MERCEDES.

An OLD SOURDOUGH watches them overload a top-heavy sled.

OLD SOURDOUGH
You've got a right smart load as it is. I wouldn't tote that tent along if I was you.

MERCEDES
Undreamed of! However in the world could I manage without a tent?

The sled runners, frozen fast, won't budge. Hal whips the dogs savagely until the sled breaks free, then overturns on the first turn, scattering gear the length of the street.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Montage: Hal doubles the dogs' rations, then cuts them to starvation when supplies run short. Six added "Outside" dogs die one by one. DUB, his shoulder ruined, is shot. Mercedes, weeping over herself more than the dogs, rides the sled despite its weight, adding to the burden.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Not only did they not know how to work dogs, but they did not know how to work themselves.

BILLEE falls in the traces and cannot rise. Hal kills him with an axe. The next day KOONA goes. Five gaunt survivors remain, Buck still nominally at the head, blind with weakness.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Spring thaw roars around them — cracking ice, rising rivers. The wretched party staggers into JOHN THORNTON's camp. Thornton, whittling an axe-handle, looks them over with quiet contempt.

JOHN THORNTON
The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
That's because you're not a fool, I suppose. All the same, we'll go on to Dawson.

Hal cracks his whip. The other dogs struggle up. Buck does not move.

Hal exchanges whip for club and beats Buck without mercy. Buck endures it without a sound, the spark of life in him nearly gone.

Thornton springs up with an inarticulate cry and hurls Hal backward.

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you.

Hal draws his hunting-knife. Thornton knocks it from his hand with the axe-handle, then cuts Buck's traces with two strokes.

The sled pulls away downriver — Mercedes riding, Hal at the gee-pole, Charles limping behind. Thornton kneels over Buck, checking for broken bones.

Out on the ice, the sled's rear end drops suddenly into a widening crack. Mercedes screams. A whole section of river gives way. Where the sled stood, only a black hole remains.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Weeks pass by the riverbank. Buck heals, tended by SKEET, a little Irish setter who cleans his wounds, and NIG, a huge, good-natured half-bloodhound. Thornton roughs Buck's head between his hands.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time.

Buck follows Thornton everywhere, lying for hours just watching his face.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yet the strain of the primitive, which the Northland had aroused in him, remained alive and active. Deep in the forest a call was sounding.

Buck lifts his head toward the treeline, listening — then the pull fades, and he returns to lie at Thornton's feet.

EXT. CLIFF EDGE - DAY

Thornton, Hans (a big placid Scandinavian) and Pete sit near a sheer three-hundred-foot drop. On a whim, Thornton sweeps his arm toward the chasm.

JOHN THORNTON
Jump, Buck!

Buck lunges for the edge in instant obedience. Thornton grapples him back just in time; Hans and Pete haul them both to safety.

PETE
It's uncanny.

JOHN THORNTON
No — it is splendid, and it is terrible, too. It sometimes makes me afraid.

INT. SALOON, CIRCLE CITY - NIGHT

BLACK BURTON picks a fight with a tenderfoot at the bar. Thornton steps between them — Burton strikes him without warning, sending him into the rail.

Buck's roar tears through the room. He is airborne before anyone can move, driving Burton to the floor, tearing his throat open before the crowd pulls him off.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A miners' meeting decided the dog had sufficient provocation. But his reputation was made.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK - DAY

Thornton's poling-boat is lining down white rapids, Hans and Pete snubbing rope from the bank. The rope checks too hard — the boat flips, and Thornton is swept downstream toward killing water.

Buck plunges in, reaches him, but the current is too strong. Thornton wedges against a rock.

JOHN THORNTON
Go, Buck! Go!

Buck is swept past, dragged ashore half-drowned. A rope is lashed to his shoulders; he's launched again, misjudges the current, is snubbed under and hauled out choking. A third time, guided true, he drives downstream like an express train and Thornton locks both arms around his neck. Hans snubs the rope; man and dog are hauled, battered, to the bank.

Thornton coughs back to life, checks Buck's ribs — three broken.

JOHN THORNTON
That settles it. We camp right here.

INT. ELDORADO SALOON - NIGHT

Men boast of their dogs' strength. MATTHEWSON, a Bonanza King, claims his dog can start seven hundred pounds.

JOHN THORNTON
Pooh, pooh. Buck can start a thousand pounds.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

He slams a sack of gold dust on the bar. Thornton has no such sum. JIM O'BRIEN, an old comrade, steps up.

JOHN THORNTON
Can you lend me a thousand?

O'BRIEN
Sure. Though it's little faith I'm having, John, that the beast can do the trick.

EXT. DAWSON MAIN STREET - DAY

A sled loaded with twenty fifty-pound sacks of flour stands frozen to the snow, sixty below zero. A crowd gathers, odds running three-to-one against Buck. A SKOOKUM KING offers to buy Buck outright before the test.

SKOOKUM KING
Gad, sir! I offer you eight hundred for him, sir, before the test.

Thornton shakes his head, kneels by Buck, rests cheek to cheek.

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck seizes his mittened hand gently between his teeth — the answer.

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck swings right, taking up the slack.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

The runners crack loose from the frozen snow.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws his whole body into the traces. The sled shudders — inch by inch — then rolls, gaining, until it moves steady down the marked hundred yards. The crowd roars. Thornton falls to his knees beside Buck, shaking with emotion.

SKOOKUM KING
Gad, sir! I'll give you a thousand for him — twelve hundred, sir.

JOHN THORNTON
No, sir. You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

EXT. WILDERNESS TRAIL - DAY

Winnings in hand, Thornton, Hans, and Pete strike east with Buck and the dogs, chasing the legend of a lost gold mine.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The months came and went, and back and forth they twisted through the uncharted vastness.

Seasons turn across the montage — summer lakeshores, blizzard divides, autumn valleys — until at last they find a shallow placer where gold shows like butter across the pan.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing for the dogs to do, and Buck spent long hours musing by the fire, and often wandered in memory with the short-legged hairy man of that other world.

EXT. FOREST CLEARING - NIGHT

Buck springs from sleep at a long, unfamiliar howl. He follows it deep into the trees and finds a lean timber WOLF, upright, calling to the sky. The wolf flees; Buck pursues, cornering him again and again until at last they touch noses in truce.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He knew he was at last answering the call, running by the side of his wood brother toward the place from where the call surely came.

They run together for miles under open sky — until Buck stops at a stream, remembering. He turns back. The wolf howls after him, mournful, fading into the distance.

Buck bursts into camp and hurls himself on Thornton in a frenzy of affection.

JOHN THORNTON
Playing the general tom-fool.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Days pass. The call sounds louder in the forest; Buck ranges farther and stays away longer, hunting alone, growing ever more wolf than dog.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a killer, a thing that preyed, living on the things that lived, unaided, alone.

JOHN THORNTON
Never was there such a dog.

PETE
When he was made, the mould was broke.

HANS
Py jingo! I t'ink so mineself.

Buck brings down a great, arrow-wounded bull moose after a four-day pursuit, then turns for home, uneasy — sensing something wrong on the wind.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Buck approaches the valley cautiously. The forest has gone silent — no birds, no squirrels. He finds Nig dead, an arrow through his body. Farther on, a sled-dog dying in the trail. Then Hans, feathered with arrows, face-down before the ruined camp.

Buck's hair rises. A gust of rage sweeps through him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For the last time in his life he allowed passion to usurp cunning and reason, because of his great love for John Thornton.

He tears through the celebrating YEEHAT hunters like a live hurricane, ripping throat after throat, scattering them in terror through the trees.

Wearied at last, Buck returns to the desolated camp. He finds Pete dead in his blankets, Skeet dead by a muddy pool — and Thornton's trail leading into the water, with no trace leading out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It left a great void in him, somewhat akin to hunger — a void which ached and ached, and which food could not fill.

EXT. VALLEY CLEARING - NIGHT

Full moon over the valley. Buck stands alone by the pool, listening. A distant yelp, then a chorus, drawing closer — the wolf pack, migrating with the moose, pours into the clearing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him.

The boldest wolf leaps at Buck; Buck breaks its neck with a single strike. Three more come and are driven back bleeding. The pack surges, and Buck is forced back against a gravel bank, fighting them all at bay.

Half an hour on, the pack draws back, exhausted. The lean gray wolf — his wood brother — steps forward and touches noses with him. An old, battle-scarred wolf follows, sits, and howls at the moon. The pack howls with him.

Buck sits back on his haunches, lifts his head, and howls with them.

He rises, and runs with the pack into the trees, side by side with the wild brother, yelping as he goes.

EXT. VALLEY - TIMELESS

Snow-bright wilderness, years later. The Yeehats' fires burn distant and small.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And here may well end the story of Buck. The years were not many when the Yeehats noted a change in the breed of timber wolves — some seen with splashes of brown on head and muzzle, and a rift of white down the chest.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They tell of a Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the pack, cunning greater than theirs, stealing from their camps, defying their bravest hunters. And there is a valley the Yeehats will not enter.

A great, gloriously coated wolf-shape crosses alone into an open glade where a thin yellow stream trickles from rotted moose-hide sacks, long grass growing up through the gold. He pauses, howls once, long and mournful — and departs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he is not always alone. When the long winter nights come on, he may be seen running at the head of the pack through the pale moonlight, leaping gigantic above his fellows, his great throat a-bellow as he sings the song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END
